High-speed data centers and backbone networks require firewalls that sustain line-rate packet classification while scaling to large rule sets with high accuracy and low hardware overhead. Existing hardware-based firewalls face trade-offs between throughput, scalability, configurability, and false-positive handling. We present HASFire, a stateless hardware-software co-designed firewall that addresses these challenges through a two-level classification architecture. A reconfigurable hardware Bloom filter performs line-rate filtering for the majority of packets using minimal resources, while a software-based Trie classifier revalidates Bloom filter matches to eliminate false positives. This design ensures that false positives do not affect the throughput of legitimate traffic and provides a bounded verification latency independent of rule set size. FPGA-based evaluation demonstrates that the hardware software co-design sustains high throughput with zero false positives.